-- The scheduling half shipped. Both halves of the item are now in place, so it
-- moves to in_test -- not done: a scheduled workflow does not fire until the
-- file is on the default branch, so the item is only really closed once this
-- reaches main and the two secrets exist.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

update public.roadmap_items
  set status = 'in_test',
      dev_percent_complete = 90,
      description = 'Two halves, both now shipped. THE REVIEW GATE SHIPPED 2026-09-09. The bug it fixed was real, not theoretical: scripts/lib/scrape-common.ts upserted `on conflict (source_url)`, which rewrote an already-published row''s specs and price on the SECOND run of any scraper with is_published left true, so a row listed on /calculators/battery could change with nobody in the loop. A SECOND VICTIM of the same overwrite had gone unnoticed: scrape-eg4.ts emits price_usd: null because EG4''s own site lists no price, and scrape-signaturesolar.ts fills that price in from the reseller -- so every EG4 re-scrape wiped the affiliate price, the field the primary revenue line depends on, off all three EG4 rows. The fix routes every scraper write through one gate: an UNPUBLISHED row is still written in place (nothing is live to protect, and it already sits in the review queue), while a PUBLISHED row''s change lands in battery_model_revisions as a proposal and the live row is not touched. proposed/previous are jsonb because a proposal is a PATCH, not a row. TWO KINDS OF SILENCE are distinguished: an absent key means the scraper has no opinion, a key present but null means the source page did not state the value -- neither ever clears a value that is already there. A partial unique index allows one open proposal per battery, so a weekly job replaces its own earlier proposal instead of stacking one per battery per week into a queue nobody reads; a proposal identical to one a reviewer already rejected is not re-raised. /admin/batteries diffs live against scraped per field and runs the battery-review.ts checks against the row AS IT WOULD BE; applying it IS the re-approval, and is_published is deliberately not a field a proposal can carry, so no proposal can publish a row. THE SCHEDULE SHIPPED 2026-09-10 as .github/workflows/scrape-batteries.yml: weekly, Monday 06:17 UTC, all five scrapers, plus a workflow_dispatch with a choice input for re-running one after a fix. Ordering matters in exactly one place -- scrape-signaturesolar.ts does not insert, it prices rows a manufacturer scrape created, so it runs last and a model EG4 added this morning is priced in the same run. Each scraper is continue-on-error with a final aggregating step, so one brand''s site being down does not cost a week of the other four. A THIRD SILENT FAILURE was found turning it on, and is the reason the schedule needed more than a YAML file. Nothing had ever inspected what a scraper returned. A source site that redesigns overnight discovers zero product URLs, parses zero products, writes zero rows, prints "Parsed 0/0 products" and EXITS 0 -- a green tick on a run that collected nothing. So did a run where every database write errored: gateWrite() returns ''failed'' and logs to stderr, but no caller had ever looked. Both are harmless while a person reads the terminal and invisible on a cron, which is the same shape as the overwrite bug: safe only because a human was watching. src/lib/scrape-health.ts is now the pure judge -- a run FAILS when discovery found nothing, when candidates were found and none parsed, when records parsed but none were written, when any write errored, or when an update-only scraper matched no existing row at all (the affiliate price silently ceasing to refresh). It WARNS without failing on partial loss, and stays silent when everything is unchanged, because specs genuinely do not move most weeks and a job that reddens for that is a job everyone learns to ignore. It sets process.exitCode rather than throwing: a stack trace is the wrong shape for "EG4''s category page is empty". Pure and in src/lib so CI gates it without the service-role key, same split as battery-revision.ts. The scrape:* npm scripts moved to --env-file-if-exists so CI needs no .env.local -- credentials arrive as env vars from repository secrets, never as a file on disk. NOT YET RUNNING: a scheduled workflow only fires from the DEFAULT BRANCH, so nothing runs until this reaches main, and the run needs repository secrets NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY set by hand in GitHub settings. Also unresolved by this item: the secrets name the single hosted Supabase project shared by staging and dev, so revisit when "Separate staging and production Supabase projects" lands -- a scheduled writer pointed at production is a different risk.'
  where title = 'Battery scraper: re-scrape scheduling + published-row review gate';

-- "Scraping stays manual-only: there is still no scheduled re-run" is no longer
-- true, and a roadmap that still says so is worse than one that never said it.
update public.roadmap_items
  set description = 'battery_models table + scripts/scrape-*.ts. Collects real battery model specs (brand, capacity, voltage, DoD) from manufacturer sites so the battery calculator can eventually recommend "4x EG4 LL-S 100Ah" instead of just a kWh number. Rows land unpublished; an admin review step (part of the admin scrapers item) gates anything reaching a visitor. Five scrapers live and wired into package.json: EG4, Victron, SunGoldPower, Signature Solar and A1 SolarStore (scrape:eg4, scrape:victron, scrape:sungoldpower, scrape:signaturesolar, scrape:a1solarstore) — Renogy was ruled out by robots.txt, which explicitly disallows AI crawlers. As of 2026-09-10 scraping is no longer manual-only: .github/workflows/scrape-batteries.yml runs all five weekly, and each scraper now judges its own run through src/lib/scrape-health.ts so a redesigned source site turns the job red instead of exiting 0 with nothing collected. The overwrite that used to make a re-run dangerous was fixed 2026-09-09 — a re-scrape no longer rewrites an already-published row, it proposes the change in battery_model_revisions and an admin applies it in /admin/batteries (see "Battery scraper: re-scrape scheduling + published-row review gate"). A weekly cloud routine to extend this same pipeline to inverters and panels is scheduled separately once repo access for it is connected.'
  where title = 'Battery spec data pipeline (scraper)';
